WEBVTT

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.539
Detection is the point where the organization notices something that might be an incident.

00:00:04.539 --> 00:00:08.319
That signal might come from a security alert, an endpoint tool, cloud monitoring,

00:00:08.319 --> 00:00:09.264
identity logs,

00:00:09.264 --> 00:00:13.548
a user report, a vendor notification, threat intelligence, a data loss signal,

00:00:13.548 --> 00:00:15.378
or an unusual business process event.

00:00:15.378 --> 00:00:17.538
Detection is not the same as confirmation.

00:00:17.538 --> 00:00:19.437
It is a prompt to ask better questions.

00:00:19.437 --> 00:00:20.252
What happened?

00:00:20.252 --> 00:00:22.957
Which system, user, service, or data set is involved?

00:00:22.957 --> 00:00:25.996
Is the activity expected, suspicious, or clearly harmful?

00:00:25.996 --> 00:00:29.528
Good detection provides context so analysts can prioritize the next step.

00:00:29.528 --> 00:00:33.385
A useful alert should point toward relevant evidence instead of just raising alarm.

00:00:33.385 --> 00:00:35.650
Weak detection creates delays and uncertainty:

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:39.334
responders may learn about an incident late, lack the logs needed to scope it,

00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:41.184
or spend time chasing noisy signals.

00:00:41.184 --> 00:00:44.374
Detection engineering should therefore focus on meaningful coverage,

00:00:44.374 --> 00:00:47.923
clear alert descriptions, useful enrichment, and routing to people who can act.

00:00:47.923 --> 00:00:49.633
The goal is not to alert on everything.

00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:53.548
The goal is to notice important signals early enough that analysis and containment still

00:00:53.548 --> 00:00:54.852
have time to reduce impact.

00:00:54.852 --> 00:00:56.852
Detection also benefits from feedback.

00:00:56.852 --> 00:01:00.702
If analysts repeatedly close an alert as noise, the detection may need tuning.

00:01:00.702 --> 00:01:04.631
If responders keep asking for context that is not present, enrichment should improve.

00:01:04.631 --> 00:01:08.060
The best detection program learns from incidents, near misses, user reports,

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.430
and false positives rather than treating alerts as fixed

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:11.440
forever.
